course: everyday
lesson: 420
title: Eyes, backs and thumbs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: After two hours of Roblox curled over his tablet like a prawn, Kai stood up and said «ow, my neck» in the voice of his grandad. His mum laughed, then showed him how to sit back with the screen held up near eye level, so his head was not hanging forward. Ollie liked to play lying on his tummy on the floor; it was comfy for twenty minutes and his back ached the whole next day. How you sit matters as much as how long you sit.
explain: Leo's thumbs throbbed after a long Tekken session and he showed them off proudly: «gamer thumbs, I'm a pro.» His mum said: «That's not a medal, that's a stop sign.» Pain is your body's message to rest, not something to play through. Priya's eyes went blurry after a long game, because people blink less when they stare at a screen and eyes get tired from looking at something close for a long time. Her dad taught her the 20-20-20 rule: every twenty minutes, look at something about twenty feet away (about six metres, like the far side of a big room) for twenty seconds.
explain: Theo thought playing in the pitch dark was cooler, like a cinema. He got headaches, and when he switched on a lamp they went away, because a bright screen in a dark room is hard on the eyes. Ruby forgot to drink anything for three hours of Minecraft and ended up tired, thirsty and headachey; now a water bottle lives next to her console. Zara's optician asked her how long she plays. Zara went red and felt a bit nervous, but her eyes were fine; the optician just said to keep taking breaks and come back for regular checks.
explain: Jayden started doing star jumps between Fortnite matches. His squad laughed at first, then saw him on camera bouncing about and joined in; now the whole squad does «jump breaks», including his cousin in Australia. Noah's dad got him a proper chair, and his back stopped aching. And Mia's rule is simple: after an hour of games she goes outside and kicks a football about, then comes back with fresh energy. A body needs running about every day as much as a brain likes games.
fact: Sitting hunched with your head bent forward over a screen strains the neck and back.
fact: Holding a screen nearer eye level and sitting back supported is easier on the neck.
fact: Staying in one position for a long time, sitting or lying, makes muscles stiff and achy.
fact: Sore thumbs or hands after playing are a sign to take a break, not a badge of skill.
fact: People blink less when staring at screens, which can make eyes feel dry and tired.
fact: The 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look at something about 20 feet away for 20 seconds.
fact: Playing with a bright screen in a dark room can cause eye strain and headaches, so a light on helps.
fact: Forgetting to drink during long games can leave you tired and headachey.
fact: Short movement breaks between games, like star jumps or a walk, keep the body fresh.
fact: Regular eye checks with an optician can catch problems early.
fact: Children need about an hour of active play every day.
fact: If pain does not go away after resting, tell a grown-up, who may take you to a doctor.
exercise: Why did Kai's neck hurt? | He sat hunched over his tablet for two hours | He slept on a very hard pillow the night before | He played a game about necks | He fell off his chair while playing
exercise: How did Kai feel when he stood up? | Stiff and old like his grandad | Fresh and full of energy | Proud of his high score | Hungry and a bit dizzy
exercise: What did Kai's mum show him? | How to sit with the screen near eye level | How to play lying down on the floor | How to turn the screen brightness right up to the top | How to hold the tablet on his knees
exercise: What happened after Ollie played lying on his tummy? | His back ached the next day | He got much better at the game | He fell asleep on the carpet | His eyes started to feel itchy
exercise: Why were Leo's sore thumbs not something to be proud of? | Pain is a sign he needed a break | Proper pros never use their thumbs | Sore thumbs make you lose the game | His mum wanted him to play longer
exercise: What did Leo's mum call his sore thumbs? | A stop sign, not a medal | A sign he was a real pro | A reward for winning a match | Nothing to worry about at all
exercise: Why did Priya's eyes go blurry? | Long screen staring makes eyes tired and dry | The game was far too colourful and bright for her eyes | Her screen was set to the wrong language | She played in a very bright room
exercise: What is the 20-20-20 rule? | Every 20 minutes, look 20 feet away for 20 seconds | Play 20 minutes, then sleep 20 minutes, then eat 20 minutes | Sit 20 inches away for 20 games a day | Blink 20 times every 20 seconds for 20 minutes
exercise: Why did Theo get headaches? | He played with a bright screen in the dark | He played with the sound turned up far too loud | He played with a lamp switched on | He played for only ten minutes
exercise: What helped Theo's headaches go away? | Switching on a lamp | Turning the screen brighter | Moving closer to the screen | Playing with his eyes half closed
exercise: Why did Ruby get tired and headachey? | She forgot to drink for three hours | She drank too much water while playing | She played sitting in a proper chair | She took too many movement breaks
exercise: How did Zara feel when the optician asked how long she plays? | Nervous and a bit embarrassed | Proud and excited to tell | Angry that she was asked | Bored and wanting to leave
exercise: What did the optician tell Zara? | Her eyes were fine; keep taking breaks | She must stop playing games for ever, starting today | She needs glasses because of games | Her eyes were damaged by her console
exercise: What happened after Jayden did star jumps between matches? | His squad copied him with «jump breaks» | His squad left him out for being silly | He was too tired to play any more | He hurt his back and had to stop
exercise: How did Noah's proper chair help? | His back stopped aching | His aim got perfect overnight | His screen became brighter | He could play all night long
exercise: What does Mia do after an hour of games? | Goes outside to play football | Starts another hour of games | Lies down on the floor to rest | Drinks a can of energy drink
exercise: If Leo's thumbs still hurt after a few days of rest, what is wise? | Tell a grown-up, who may take him to a doctor | Keep playing every day to make the thumbs stronger | Ignore it, thumbs always mend | Wrap them up and play with his feet
test: «my neck's killing me, been on Roblox since lunch, like this» (head bent right down). What helps most? | Sit back with the screen nearer eye level | Bend the head even further down | Play lying flat on the floor instead, on his tummy | Hold the tablet on the knees
test: Leo: «gamer thumbs, they ache, means I'm a pro». What's true? | Aching thumbs mean he needs a break | Aching thumbs prove he is skilled | Real pros play through any pain | Thumbs only hurt for beginners
test: Priya, rubbing her eyes: «everything's blurry, is my telly broken?» Most likely? | Her eyes are tired from long staring | The telly has a broken screen | She needs a much bigger screen | She has been blinking too much
test: Theo: «lights off is way more epic, like a cinema». What does the lesson say? | A lamp on helps stop eye strain | Darkness is best for the eyes | Lights make games harder to see | Cinemas prove the dark is healthy
test: Ruby, three hours in: «why've I got a headache, I've just been sitting». What did she forget? | To drink some water | To turn the sound up | To sit closer to the screen | To eat a packet of sweets
test: Squad on voice chat: «Jayden, why are you bouncing?? lol». What is he doing? | A movement break between matches | Showing off that he won the match | Celebrating that the game crashed | Trying to warm up his console
test: Mate: «20-20-20 is made up, nobody does that». What is the rule really for? | Giving the eyes regular short rests | Making games last twenty minutes | Teaching kids to count to twenty | Measuring how far the TV should be
test: Mum: «You've been on for an hour, out you go, kick a ball.» Mia: «fine». What did Mia find? | She came back with fresh energy | She forgot how to play the game | She was too tired to play again | Nothing changed at all
test: Ollie: «floor's comfier than a chair». What happened the next day? | His back ached | His back felt stronger | His eyes felt sharper | His thumbs stopped hurting
test: Teacher: «Which is the healthiest way to play for an hour?» | Sitting well, with water and a break | Lying on the floor in the dark | Hunched close to the screen, no break | Standing on one leg the whole time
test: Zara: «the optician asked how long I play, I'm so busted». What's true? | Eye checks help catch problems early | Opticians report kids to their schools | Opticians only see people with glasses | The question means her eyes are ruined
exam: State the content of the rule known as «20-20-20». | At intervals of twenty minutes, an object some twenty feet distant is regarded for twenty seconds | At intervals of twenty seconds, the eyes are closed for twenty minutes | Play is restricted to twenty minutes on twenty days | The screen is placed at a distance of twenty inches for twenty games
exam: Identify the principal reason for which discomfort in the thumbs following prolonged play ought to be heeded. | It signifies that the body requires rest | It indicates a high level of proficiency | It is necessary for improvement at the game | It is invariably of no significance
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises play upon a bright screen in an unlit room? | It may occasion eye strain and headaches | It is the condition most beneficial to the eyes | It enhances the clarity of vision permanently | It is without any effect upon the eyes
exam: It is given that a pupil has been seated before a screen for one hour. Select the measure most consistent with the established facts. | A period of physical activity, such as outdoor play | A further hour of play without interruption of any kind | A reduction of the room's illumination | The adoption of a prone position upon the floor
